#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""apply_batch19_step2.py - Step 2: reminder reliability.

Turns these red tests green:

  test_editing_recurring_to_one_off_clears_expire_at
  test_ttl_grace_survives_a_worker_outage
  test_failed_state_write_does_not_resend
  test_event_recovers_after_a_transient_outage   (rewritten, see below)
  test_retry_backs_off
  test_sending_a_reminder_touches_updated_at

and brings two new ones with it:

  test_blocked_user_stops_being_retried
  test_health_report_sees_a_permanently_failed_reminder

What changes:

  app/utils/dates.py        expire_for_repeat now leaves one period plus at
                            least 30 days. A daily event used to expire two
                            days after its reminder, and only a successful
                            send pushed that forward, so a worker outage
                            longer than the gap let the TTL index delete the
                            user's events.

  app/services/events.py    Editing a recurring event into a one-off now
                            $unsets expire_at. $set alone left the old marker
                            behind and the TTL deleted a live event.

  app/services/reminders.py Four changes in one function:
                            - a delivered occurrence is recorded as
                              last_sent_key, so a state write that fails
                              after a successful send no longer causes a
                              duplicate reminder;
                            - failures back off (1, 5, 15, 60, 180, 360 min)
                              instead of retrying on the very next pass;
                            - telegram.error.Forbidden is the only terminal
                              failure. Everything else keeps retrying up to
                              MAX_TRANSIENT_ATTEMPTS, so a Telegram outage no
                              longer silences an event for good;
                            - every success path writes updated_at.

  app/services/health.py    measure() counts failed reminders and reports
                            them. A blocked bot used to look identical to a
                            healthy queue.

One test was rewritten rather than simply passed. The old
test_event_recovers_after_a_telegram_outage asserted an internal precondition
("after five failures the row is 'failed'") that only held because of the bug.
Under the new design a transient error never reaches that state, so the test
now asserts the behaviour instead: while Telegram is down nothing is
delivered, and once it recovers the reminder goes out exactly once. The
terminal case moved into its own test, using a real Forbidden.

Usage:
    python apply_batch19_step2.py --check   # resolve everything, write nothing
    python apply_batch19_step2.py           # apply

Every file is replaced whole and guarded by the hash of its Step 1 version,
so the script refuses rather than clobbering anything you changed by hand.
Reads and writes are UTF-8 with LF, and writes go through a temp file and an
atomic replace.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import hashlib
import os
import sys
from pathlib import Path

ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parent


def read(path: Path) -> str:
    raw = path.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
    return raw.replace("\r\n", "\n").replace("\r", "\n")


def write_atomic(path: Path, content: str) -> None:
    tmp = path.with_suffix(path.suffix + ".tmp")
    with open(tmp, "w", encoding="utf-8", newline="\n") as handle:
        handle.write(content)
    os.replace(tmp, path)


def sha(text: str) -> str:
    return hashlib.sha256(text.encode("utf-8")).hexdigest()[:16]


